Covid19-Demographic-Analyzer
✅ Prerequisites

Before you set up and run the project, ensure your machine has the following installed:

Node.js (LTS version recommended, e.g. ≥ 16.x)

npm (comes bundled with Node.js)

MongoDB (local MongoDB server, or a remote MongoDB URI)

A code editor (e.g. Visual Studio Code)

Optionally, you may also want to have tools like git (to clone the repo) and a Postman-like tool (for testing API endpoints).

🛠️ Setup & Installation (on another PC)

Follow these steps to get the project up and running:

# 1. Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/PrashoHaran/Covid19-Demographic-Analyzer.git  
cd Covid19-Demographic-Analyzer

# 2. Install dependencies for backend
cd backend  
npm install  

# 3. Install dependencies for frontend (if applicable)
cd ../frontend  
npm install  

# 4. Configure environment variables
#    Copy the example env file (if exists) and update with your configuration.
#    For example:
cp ../backend/.env.example ../backend/.env  

#    Then open ../backend/.env and set values like:
#    MONGODB_URI = your-mongodb-connection-string
#    PORT = 3000   (or your preferred port)
#    (Any other variables your project requires)

# 5. Start the backend server
cd ../backend  
npm start        # or “npm run dev” if you use nodemon

# 6. Start the frontend (if applicable)
cd ../frontend  
npm start

# 7. Open the application
#    Go to http://localhost:<PORT> in your browser (e.g. http://localhost:3000)

📁 Project Structure (after cloning)
Covid19-Demographic-Analyzer/
├── backend/        # Node.js + Express server, API endpoints, database logic
├── frontend/       # Frontend UI (HTML/CSS/JS) for user interaction
├── README.md       # Project documentation
└── .gitignore      # Files/folders to ignore in version control

🔧 Configuration

MongoDB connection:

In backend/.env, set MONGODB_URI to either your local MongoDB instance (e.g. mongodb://localhost:27017/yourdbname) or a remote MongoDB service URI.

Port configuration:

If your server uses environment variable for port (e.g. PORT), confirm that port isn’t already in use.

CORS / Frontend origin:

If your frontend and backend run on different ports (or domains), ensure that CORS settings in backend allow requests from your frontend origin.

🚀 Running & Testing

Use a REST client (e.g., Postman) or frontend UI to send requests.

Typical endpoints: POST /submit to fetch data from public APIs and store in MongoDB; GET /records to retrieve stored data.

Confirm that MongoDB is running and connection string is correct before starting the server.
 